          "Welcome to HSE Fundamentals. HSE stands for Health, Safety, and Environment, and if you're taking this course with Nigeria's oil and gas sector in mind, you've picked one of the industries where HSE isn't a nice-to-have policy binder gathering dust in an office — it's the difference between a shift that ends normally and one that doesn't.\n\n## What HSE Actually Covers\n\nHealth covers the physical and mental wellbeing of workers, including long-term exposure risks like noise, chemicals, or repetitive strain, not just visible injuries. Safety covers the prevention of accidents and incidents that could cause injury or death, from a slip on a wet deck to a catastrophic process failure. Environment covers the prevention and management of harm to the surrounding air, water, soil, and communities from a company's operations. On an oil and gas site, these three are constantly intertwined: a gas leak is simultaneously a safety hazard, a health hazard, and an environmental hazard, all from one event.\n\n## Key Terms You'll Use Constantly\n\nA **hazard** is anything with the potential to cause harm — a hazard doesn't have to have hurt anyone yet to be real. A **risk** is the likelihood that a hazard will actually cause harm, combined with how severe that harm would be. An **incident** is any unplanned event that resulted in, or could have resulted in, injury, illness, damage, or environmental harm. A **near-miss** is an incident that almost happened but didn't — no one was hurt, nothing was damaged, but the conditions for a real incident were all present. The single most important habit this course will try to build in you is treating near-misses as seriously as actual incidents. A near-miss is free information about how the next real incident will happen, if nothing changes.\n\n## Why This Matters More in Oil and Gas Than Almost Anywhere Else\n\nOil and gas operations combine several hazard categories that most industries only deal with one or two of at a time: flammable and explosive materials, high pressure systems, heavy rotating equipment, working at height, confined spaces, road transport between remote locations, and often sites far from immediate emergency care. That combination is exactly why the sector runs on formal HSE systems rather than informal common sense — common sense doesn't scale across a workforce of thousands, across contractors, across night shifts, across years of operation. A formal system does.\n\n## Bringing It Together\n\nThis lesson introduced HSE as three connected disciplines — Health, Safety, and Environment — and the core vocabulary of hazard, risk, incident, and near-miss that the rest of this course builds on. The next lesson looks specifically at how HSE functions inside Nigeria's oil and gas industry: who regulates it, what makes the Nigerian context distinct, and why HSE performance has become a real factor in who gets hired.",
