          "Before any risk can be assessed, it has to be identified — and oil and gas sites present a genuinely wide range of hazard categories, often several at once in the same location. This lesson works through the main categories you'll be trained to recognize.\n\n## Physical Hazards\n\nPhysical hazards include noise from rotating equipment and compressors, vibration, extreme heat from flaring or equipment, working at height on rigs and platforms, and moving machinery. On a drilling rig or production platform, physical hazards are often the most visible category, but not always the most dangerous — that distinction usually belongs to process safety hazards.\n\n## Process Safety Hazards\n\nThese are hazards tied to the loss of containment of hazardous materials — a pipe failure, a valve that doesn't hold, a pressure vessel exceeding its limits. Process safety hazards are what turn routine operations into major incidents: fires, explosions, and toxic releases almost always trace back to a process safety failure somewhere in the chain. This category gets its own dedicated attention across the industry precisely because when it goes wrong, it tends to go wrong catastrophically rather than in small, contained ways.\n\n## Chemical Hazards\n\nHydrocarbons themselves, along with drilling fluids, produced water, and hydrogen sulfide (H2S) gas, which is both toxic and, at certain concentrations, undetectable by smell despite its usual rotten-egg odor at lower concentrations. H2S specifically is treated as one of the most serious chemical hazards in upstream operations, because exposure at high concentrations can be fatal within minutes, and because the sense you'd normally rely on to detect it — smell — actually fails you at the most dangerous concentrations.\n\n## Occupational Health Hazards\n\nThis category covers the hazards that harm workers gradually, over shifts and years, rather than in a single dramatic event — and on a Nigerian site it deserves just as much attention as the more visible categories above. **Noise** from compressors, generators, and rotating equipment causes cumulative, often irreversible hearing damage well before it ever feels dangerous in the moment, which is exactly why exposure needs to be measured and controlled, not judged by ear. **Heat stress** is a genuine, everyday risk in Nigeria's tropical climate, compounded by flame-resistant coveralls and PPE that trap body heat during physically demanding tasks — dehydration and heat exhaustion are common, preventable, and easy to dismiss as someone just having a rough day rather than a hazard needing a control. **Fatigue** builds up across long shift rotations and remote camp postings, and is a documented contributing factor in a meaningful share of serious industrial incidents, not just a comfort issue. **Manual handling** — lifting pipe sections, chemical drums, or sacks of drilling fluid additives — causes a steady stream of back and joint injuries when done without proper technique or mechanical aids. **Ergonomic hazards**, like awkward postures in a control room or repetitive valve operation, add up over a career even though no single instance looks alarming. **Chemical exposure and respiratory hazards** come from produced hydrocarbons, drilling additives, and welding fumes, and are managed through a combination of ventilation, PPE, and exposure monitoring. Taken together, these are usually managed under a facility's **occupational hygiene programme** — the ongoing, structured monitoring of noise levels, air quality, and worker health that catches problems building up long before anyone gets visibly hurt.\n\n## Bringing It Together\n\nThis lesson worked through five hazard categories present on oil and gas sites: physical, process safety, chemical, occupational health, and — as the next lesson's risk assessment process will help you weigh — the psychosocial pressures of remote and high-consequence work. Recognizing which category a given hazard falls into is the first step toward managing it correctly — a process safety hazard needs a fundamentally different response than an occupational health one. The next lesson takes this list of hazard types and applies a formal method for assessing how much risk each one actually represents.",
